WEBVTT

c01
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.376
Remember the two cases? Different decades. Same lesson.

c02
00:00:07.864 --> 00:00:10.854
Map the process first and only then change it.

c03
00:00:11.384 --> 00:00:18.270
Now let's apply it. This is the HR case from Lesson 1, a services company with about 400 employees.

c04
00:00:18.508 --> 00:00:21.418
The HR director wants a chatbot on the HR inbox.

c05
00:00:21.656 --> 00:00:27.102
Live next month. Three roles matter here. The HR director who asked for the chatbot.

c06
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:33.278
The HR staff who answer employees every day. And the HR manager who owns the policies.

c07
00:00:33.766 --> 00:00:37.144
HR gets about 90 requests a month. Most of them?

c08
00:00:37.482 --> 00:00:41.056
Repeats. Vacation days, pension, expenses, overtime.

c09
00:00:41.294 --> 00:00:44.204
They arrive by WhatsApp, by email, and in person.

c10
00:00:44.442 --> 00:00:49.080
There's no system. And nobody knows how many of them actually need human judgment.

c11
00:00:49.318 --> 00:00:53.346
The tempting first move is a chatbot. Not yet.

c12
00:00:53.834 --> 00:00:58.764
First, we map the process as it is. How it actually happens on a normal day.

c13
00:00:59.002 --> 00:01:02.858
No fixing. No AI. Field one is the process name.

c14
00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:07.114
Plain and specific. Answering employees' repeat questions to HR.

c15
00:01:07.602 --> 00:01:11.980
Field two is the trigger. The concrete thing that starts the process.

c16
00:01:12.218 --> 00:01:17.144
A message. A form. A date. Before I fill it in, you choose.

c17
00:01:19.554 --> 00:01:24.292
All three channels go on the map. Because all three really happen.

c18
00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:28.120
Where a process starts and where it ends is a decision.

c19
00:01:28.358 --> 00:01:32.536
Without clear boundaries, it swells until it covers the whole organization.

c20
00:01:33.024 --> 00:01:36.342
Field three is the steps, each one with a role owner.

c21
00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:40.526
Here, there are three. Receiving the request, checking the topic,

c22
00:01:40.584 --> 00:01:46.062
and then an answer or an escalation. Owners are roles, not names.

c23
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.630
That way, the map stays true, even when people change.

c24
00:01:49.868 --> 00:01:54.066
Each role gets its own lane. So you can always see who is holding the work.

c25
00:01:54.304 --> 00:01:58.242
Field four asks where responsibility passes, from one role to another.

c26
00:02:00.212 --> 00:02:05.430
from HR staff to the HR manager, when a question goes beyond known policy.

c27
00:02:05.668 --> 00:02:08.118
That's the most dangerous moment in a process.

c28
00:02:08.356 --> 00:02:11.606
The staffer assumes the manager knows what happened before.

c29
00:02:11.844 --> 00:02:16.598
A good map marks each handoff. From whom, to whom, what passes,

c30
00:02:16.656 --> 00:02:19.146
and what happens if the receiver is unavailable.

c31
00:02:19.384 --> 00:02:25.310
Here, there's no single channel for it. Field five is knowledge sources.

c32
00:02:25.548 --> 00:02:28.606
The vacation, pension, expense, and overtime policies.

c33
00:02:28.844 --> 00:02:33.138
Each one gets an owner, here, the HR manager, and a sensitivity tag.

c34
00:02:33.376 --> 00:02:36.090
Public, internal, confidential, or restricted.

c35
00:02:36.578 --> 00:02:41.696
These policies are internal, but an employee's personal or financial details are confidential.

c36
00:02:41.934 --> 00:02:47.012
They never go into a public AI tool. Field six is existing controls.

c37
00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.620
Is anything checking the answers before they go out?

c38
00:02:49.858 --> 00:02:51.536
So, what goes in the map?

c39
00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:56.984
No formal control. It depends on the staffer's own judgment.

c40
00:02:57.222 --> 00:03:01.320
And that's a valid answer. It's exactly what mapping reveals.

c41
00:03:01.808 --> 00:03:06.234
Field seven is gaps. And here you can simply write: unclear.

c42
00:03:06.472 --> 00:03:08.662
Nobody knows how many wrong answers were given.

c43
00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:12.978
And there's no single handoff channel. That's a diagnostic result.

c44
00:03:13.466 --> 00:03:20.304
Field eight is the success metric. Today, about 35% of the HR team's time goes to repeat questions.

c45
00:03:20.792 --> 00:03:25.638
The target? 20%. Within three months. A number you can check later.

c46
00:03:28.988 --> 00:03:30.898
Next, you fill in field five yourself.
